      "height": 3352,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Alan Wilson from Stilton, Peterborough, Cambs, UK",
      "description": "Royal Air Force (RAF) BAC Jet Provost T5A registration XW323, c/n EEP/JP/987.  Built 1970 and delivered in December to RAF Cranwell.  During 1971 it was one of two Jet Provosts allocated for pilot training of Prince Charles (Flt Lt The Prince of Wales) and was known as 'Golden Eagle Two'.  It remained in use at Cranwell until 1991, when it moved to No. 1 Flying Training School RAF (1FTS) at RAF Linton-on-Ouse, from where it retired in October 1992.  In December of that year it was transported to Hendon where it remains on display in the Historic Main Hangars, RAF Museum, Hendon, London, UK.  22-3-2015.",
      "dateCreated": "2015-03-22 12:56",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "raf-cranwell-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcrt",
